    "slot_number": "slot-9-2-dyn-4",
    "slot_key": "Batch update processing and status handling",
    "slot_value": "Batch updates should be processed as quickly as the system can handle them, but not leave records half-updated without a clear status. Large imports or mass corrections should run as a queued process with a completion result rather than as a normal single update. If a queued batch update is only partially applied, the system must clearly report that it is partial and show which records succeeded, failed, and were skipped, along with error reasons. If a batch would change items that are actively being sold or counted, it should pause or be blocked rather than forcing through. It should wait if there is an open sales session, a live checkout in progress, or a critical inventory count happening on those items. Once the sales window closes or the conflicting activity is resolved, it can continue, and staff should be told why it was held up. It should resume only when the conflicting activity is fully closed out and the affected inventory is no longer in an active transaction or count. There is no fixed time limit defined yet; if it sits too long, staff should get an alert and manually decide whether to keep waiting, cancel it, or process it another way. Sensitive or high-impact changes should trigger alerts sooner and usually have a shorter tolerance for waiting. Anything that affects sellable stock in a busy department or touches high-value or unique items should notify staff quickly if it is held up, while lower-risk housekeeping changes can wait longer. A simple note update is less urgent than anything that changes quantity, availability, or POS-facing stock.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

